WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.573
File names and paths are input, even when they look like ordinary strings.

2
00:00:05.573 --> 00:00:17.576
If a user, configuration file, environment variable, archive, or network message can influence a path, the program must decide what locations are allowed before opening or writing anything.

3
00:00:17.576 --> 00:00:28.721
Path traversal occurs when input can move access outside the intended directory, often through dot-dot segments, absolute paths, drive-specific syntax, encoded separators, or platform-specific path behavior.

4
00:00:28.721 --> 00:00:36.009
A path that looks harmless before normalization may resolve somewhere else after the operating system interprets it.

5
00:00:36.009 --> 00:00:43.725
A strong path handling design starts with an allowlisted base directory and a clear rule for relative input.

6
00:00:43.725 --> 00:00:53.155
Canonicalization can help compare the resolved path to the allowed root, but it must be used carefully and close to the operation.

7
00:00:53.155 --> 00:00:59.586
Symbolic links and time-of-check to time-of-use races can matter when attackers can modify the filesystem.

8
00:00:59.586 --> 00:01:03.872
Avoid treating a string check as the whole security decision.

9
00:01:03.872 --> 00:01:12.446
If the program needs to create files, consider safe creation flags, restrictive permissions, and behavior when the file already exists.

10
00:01:12.446 --> 00:01:17.590
Extensions are useful for user experience, but they are weak security evidence.

11
00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:21.019
An attacker can rename a file.

12
00:01:21.019 --> 00:01:24.449
A malformed file can carry a normal extension.

13
00:01:24.449 --> 00:01:29.593
When content matters, validate the actual content, expected header, or parser result.

14
00:01:29.593 --> 00:01:38.166
Also enforce file size limits before reading an entire file into memory, and handle errors without leaking sensitive filesystem details.

15
00:01:38.166 --> 00:01:43.310
Path input should lead to a narrow, predictable set of filesystem operations.

16
00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:50.598
When the program cannot prove a path is within policy, the secure answer is to reject it.
